  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226602/assets/f556adb5f1af77ed89db28638a19051b6ebb8ff056eabe60a8a6357984011a12.png)\n\nThe circles $r=1$ and $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $B?$",
  "worked_solution": "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={60}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={300}^{∘}.$  \n\nAlso, notice that $r=1$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $B$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(1,{300}^{∘}).$",
